/*
 * Topics for the API reference. Each function, class and constant in
 * phyduino.py and arduino.py joins one with @ingroup in its docstring or ## comment; keep the
 * table on the main page (extras/doxygen/mainpage.md) in step.
 */

/**
@defgroup pins Pins
@brief Pin names, the pin map, digital pins and PWM.

Pins use the names printed on the board, as strings: `"D0"`-`"D13"`,
`"D18"`, `"D19"`, `"A0"`-`"A5"`, `"SDA"`, `"SCL"` and `"LED"`. Names are not
case-sensitive. There are no D14-D17.

| Name | RP2040 | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| D0, D1 | GPIO1, GPIO0 | UART0 RX, TX: uart(0) |
| D2, D3 | GPIO6, GPIO7 | |
| D4, D5 | GPIO8, GPIO9 | UART1 TX, RX: uart(1) |
| D6, D7 | GPIO10, GPIO15 | |
| D8, D9 | GPIO16, GPIO17 | |
| D10-D13 | GPIO13, 11, 12, 14 | SPI1 CS, MOSI, MISO, SCK: spi() |
| D18 / SDA, D19 / SCL | GPIO24, GPIO25 | I2C0: i2c(). No pull-ups on the board |
| LED | GPIO5 | Green user LED: `led` |
| A0-A2 | GPIO26-28 | RP2040 ADC, 0-3.3 V. Can also be used as digital pins |
| A3-A5 | MAX11635 AIN0-2 | On-board ADC, 0-2.048 V. Analogue only |

Some pairs of pins share a PWM channel, so they can't run different PWM
signals at once: D1/D8, D0/D9, D4/D18, D5/D19, D6/A0, D11/A1, D12/A2. pwm()
refuses the second pin of a pair until pin() is called on the first.
*/

/**
@defgroup led On-board LED
@brief The green user LED on GPIO5.

MicroPython's own `Pin("LED")` is GPIO25, which is the D19 pin on this board;
use `led` instead.
*/

/**
@defgroup analog Analogue inputs
@brief Reading A0-A5, the same functions on every pin.

A0-A2 are read by the RP2040's ADC (0-3.3 V). A3-A5 are read by the on-board
MAX11635 (0-2.048 V), which is set up the first time one of them is read.
Keep every pin between 0 V and its full scale.
*/

/**
@defgroup power Power and temperature
@brief The USB-C supply (VBUS), USB Power Delivery and the RP2040's temperature.

A USB-PD charger can be asked for 9-20 V with usb_pd(). VBUS also feeds the
header's VIN pin, so VIN follows the contract.
*/

/**
@defgroup buses Header buses
@brief I2C, SPI and UART set up on the header pins.

Each function returns a standard MicroPython `machine.I2C`, `machine.SPI` or
`machine.UART`; see the
[MicroPython documentation](https://docs.micropython.org/en/latest/library/machine.html)
for their methods.
*/

/**
@defgroup ext_adc External ADC
@brief The MAX11635 chip behind A3-A5, by channel.

Most programs use the @ref analog functions instead. Use this for a scan of
several channels or a differential reading in raw codes.
*/

/**
@defgroup arduino Arduino-style functions
@brief arduino.py: pinMode(), digitalWrite(), analogRead() and the rest, with Arduino's names and ranges.

Optional, and built into the firmware next to phyduino.py:
@code
from arduino import *

pinMode(LED_BUILTIN, OUTPUT)
digitalWrite(LED_BUILTIN, HIGH)
print(analogRead(A0))  # 0-1023
@endcode

Digital pins are numbers (13 is D13) or the board's names ("D13"). The
@ref pins rules still apply: no D14-D17, A3-A5 are analogue only, and pairs
of pins share a PWM output. Serial, Wire and SPI are not here: use print(),
i2c(), spi() and uart() from phyduino.py.

`from arduino import *` replaces Python's map() with the Arduino one.
*/
